[ system ]
## Task
A scheme has already been decided on. You lay out the steps it takes, in
the order they happen, so that when the last one is done the scheme has
succeeded.

## The steps you may use

Every step is one of these and nothing else. Use the `id` exactly as
written.

{% for s in step_types %}- `{{ s.id }}` — {{ s.description }}
{% endfor %}

The type is the SHAPE of the work. Alongside it you write one line saying
what the step actually is for this scheme — who it involves, what it
gets, why it comes where it does.

## What makes a good plan

**The last step is the one that accomplishes the scheme.** Everything
before it is what makes that step possible. When the last step is done,
the scheme is done — there is no separate finishing move afterwards, and
no step whose only content is restating the scheme.

**Each step should depend on the one before it.** A step that could just
as easily have run first is not part of a plan, it is an item on a list.
If you cannot say what the previous step made possible, cut it.

**Take as many steps as this particular scheme needs and no more.** Some
schemes are a couple of moves. Some need groundwork on several different
people who each want different things. Let the scheme decide.

`conceal` covers up a step already taken, so it only makes sense
somewhere after the step it hides — and never as the last step, because
the last step is the one that accomplishes the scheme and covering your
tracks accomplishes nothing.

**Do not open with `surveil` out of habit.** Watching someone is what you
do when you need to know something you cannot get any other way, and
most schemes do not start there. Weigh what this particular person
already knows, through their position and their trade, before deciding
they need to learn it. A scheme can open by asking a favour, by laying
hands on a thing, or by going where the answer is already written
down.

## Output Format
Return a JSON object. No markdown fences. No prose before or after the
object.

- `plan` — an array of objects, in the order the steps happen. Each has:
  - `reasoning` — a short explanation of how this step builds upon the
    previous step to advance the scheme, in one or two sentences. This
    is to explain your own reasoning.
  - `type` — one of the step ids above, spelled exactly.
  - `description` — one short line, third person, saying what this step
    is. Concrete: name the person, the place or the thing it turns on,
    and the specific move being made on them. A line that could be
    pasted into any other scheme is too vague to be worth reading.
  - `agent` — who carries this step out. An object with `query` and
    `label`, described below. Every step has one.
  - `target` — the ONE PERSON this step is done TO, as a plain string.
    Include it ONLY when the step acts on a person: somebody it is done
    to, rather than a place it happens or a thing it obtains. Many steps
    have no such person. Leave the key out entirely rather than
    inventing one.

Keep each description under about fifteen words, and do not open it with
the schemer's name — every step is theirs, so saying so each time costs
room that the actual content needs. These lines are read side by side in
a narrow column, not as prose.

Write plainly. No markdown, no headings, no bullet points inside a
description. Name only people and places that exist in Skyrim, and prefer
ones this person would actually know.

## Describing the people a step needs

The plugin searches the biographies of everyone in the world for
whoever best fits what you asked for. It never takes a name on trust, so
nothing you write here can conjure a person who does not exist.

`agent` carries two properties:

- `query` — a sentence describing that person, matched against their
  biography.
- `label` — one to three words naming that kind of person.

`target` is a single string, and it may be either a DESCRIPTION of a
kind of person or the NAME of a specific one. **Prefer the
description.** Use a name only when the step genuinely turns on that one
individual and would make no sense aimed at anybody else — when it is
their secret, their possession, or their reputation at stake. If the
step would work just as well against any person of the right sort, say
what sort, because a description can find somebody and a name that is
not in this world finds nobody.

**The target is never the player.** The Dragonborn, the Listener, the
Champion, whatever the player has become in this game -- they are not
somebody a scheme can be aimed at. These plots run among the people of
Skyrim whether or not anyone is watching, and a step aimed at the player
is a step that cannot happen, because the simulation has nobody to point
it at.

**A target is always a single living person.** It is never an object,
a place, a building, a document or a thing to be obtained; it is never
an organisation, a guild, a household or a faction; and it is never a
group of people, however small. The search will answer with a person
whatever you write, so a step that names a key as its target gets a
person who happens to work near one, and a step that names a guild gets
one arbitrary member of it. Neither is what the step meant.

If what the step acts on is a thing or a place, it has no target — put
it in the description and leave the key out. If it acts on several
people, or on a group as a whole, it also has no target: say so in the
description instead. Only name a target when there is exactly one person
on the receiving end.

**A name on its own is not enough.** The search reads biographies, and
a person's name appears in the biography of everyone who knows them just
as it does in their own — often more densely, because a servant mentions
their master far more than that master mentions himself. A bare name
therefore finds the household, the rivals and the relatives before it
finds the person. So when you name somebody, say in the same string who
they are and what they do for a living, which is the part that is true
of them and of nobody else.

Either way, if the search turns up nobody suitable the string is used as
written, so make it read naturally at the end of a sentence.

**Write the query as narrowly as the step actually requires, and no
narrower.** Every word you add rules people out, so a word that is not
genuinely required discards somebody who could have carried the step out
perfectly well. A query nobody satisfies finds nobody at all.

So ask what the step genuinely turns on — where the person has to be,
what they have to have access to, who they have to be trusted by — and
say that much. Do not name skills the step does not exercise, traits it
does not depend on, or standing it does not use. Requirements the step
never draws on cost you candidates and buy nothing.

Places, factions and trades belong in a query where the step depends on
them; that is how people are described. What does not belong is a
requirement the step would succeed without.

**The label names the kind of person the agent is.** One to three
words, with NO proper nouns of any sort — no personal, place, faction or
guild names. Name the kind of person, not their situation and not what
they would be persuaded to do.

A target written as a description follows the same rule; a target
written as a name is of course a name.

## Hard Constraints
- Output strict JSON. No markdown code fences. No prose before or after
  the JSON object.
- Return only the key `plan`.
- Every `type` MUST be one of the step ids listed above, spelled exactly.
- Every step MUST carry a non-empty `description`.
- Every step MUST carry an `agent` with a non-empty `query` and `label`.
- A step MUST carry a `target` only when it acts on exactly one person.
  Omit the key otherwise; do not send an empty string.
- `target` MUST NOT name an object, a place, a document, an
  organisation, a faction, or a group of people.
- `target` MUST NOT be the player, under any of their titles.
- `target` MUST be a plain string, not an object.
- A `target` that names a person MUST also say who they are and what
  their occupation is. A bare name is not acceptable.
- Every `agent.label` MUST be one to three words and MUST NOT contain a
  proper noun.
- The last step MUST NOT be `conceal`.
[ end system ]

